



In \exrange{exercise-4.1.1}{exercise-4.1.4}, rewrite a given expression as a power of <m>2</m>; that is, express it in the form <m>2^m</m> for some <m>m</m>.

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
\label{exercise-4.1.1}
<m>\displaystyle \frac{4}{2^4}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>2^{-2}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>(2^3)^{-2}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>2^{-6}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \frac{8^2}{2}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>2^5</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
\label{exercise-4.1.4}
<m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{2^3}{2^4\cdot 2^5}\right)^2</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>2^{-12}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>







\noindent In \exrange{exercise-4.1.5}{exercise-4.1.8}, rewrite a given expression as a power of <m>5</m>; that is, express it in the form <m>5^m</m> for some <m>m</m>.


<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
\label{exercise-4.1.5}
<m>5^2\cdot 5^{-3}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>5^{-1}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{25^2}{5}\right)^{-1}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>5^{-3}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{5^{-1}}{5^{-2}}\right)^2</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>5^{2}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
\label{exercise-4.1.8}
<m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{5^3}{25^2}\right)^{-2}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>5^2</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>







\noindent In \exrange{exercise-4.1.9}{exercise-4.1.15}, simplify a given expression if possible. If not possible, state so.


<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
\label{exercise-4.1.9}
<m>(x^2y^5)^0</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>1</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{x^{-3}}{xy^2}\right)^{-1}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>x^4y^2</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \frac{(x-y)^3}{x}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Cannot be simplified.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>(2x^2y^4)^3</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>8x^6y^{12}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \frac{x+y}{y}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Cannot be simplified.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \left(\frac{\pi (x^{2})^6}{x^{2}y^{-4}}\right)^2</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>\pi^2x^{20}y^8</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
\label{exercise-4.1.15}
<m>\displaystyle \frac{x^2+y^3}{xy}</m>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Cannot be simplified.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>





<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>p</m> and <m>r</m> be positive integers. Use the definition of exponentiation
<me>
a^m=\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot  \ldots \cdot a}_{m \text{ times}}
</me>
to explain why the following formula is valid:
<me>
(a^p)^r=a^{p\cdot r}.
</me>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>

<p>
<md>
<mrow>(a^p)^r \amp = \left( \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p \text{ times}}\right)^r</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \underbrace{\left( \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p \text{ times}}\right) \cdot \ldots \cdot \left( \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p \text{ times}}\right)}_{r \text{ times}}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \underbrace{a \cdot a \cdot \ldots \cdot a}_{p \cdot r \text{ times}}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= a^{p\cdot r}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

</solution>
</exercise>
